gm-method (a code-probe says a VISIBLE thing is absent - distrust the probe field, not the render): reading an instanced-mesh library's active instance count via the raw `.instancesCount`/`.count` property is the WRONG accessor for a mesh that uses LODs - it can read 0 or stale even while the mesh renders many instances (the lib tracks active/visible instances through a different field/path). Concluding 'zero instances -> the object is absent/broken' from that property burned a whole debugging turn chasing a non-existent 'trees have no instances' bug that a single SCREENSHOT immediately disproved (dense foliage on screen). GENERAL: when an in-code probe claims something the user can SEE is missing/zero, the probe is suspect before the renderer is - cross-check with a pixel witness (screenshot / readback) FIRST, and find the library's real active-count accessor before asserting absence. A live visual witness outranks a property read for 'is it actually there'.